south africa has deployed its military  to stop violence sparked by the jailing  of former president jacob zuma  at least six people have died hundreds  more have been arrested  south africa's highest court has begun  hearing zuma's appeal against his  15-month prison sentence for contempt  president siobha ramaphosa has called  for calm  warning the unrest could lead to  shortages of food and medicine  soldiers guard the streets of south  africa they're there to protect police  after protests marked by looting and  destruction of property  became more violent loot has plundered a  large shopping mall in durban overnight  it's one of many businesses in the port  city that were ransacked over the  weekend  another shopping mall lies in ruins in  peter martinsburg  some 80 kilometers from durban  the unrest has been mainly concentrated  in the southeastern province of  kwazulu-natal  zuma's home region many of his  supporters there are angered by zuma's  15-month jail term  imposed for failing to appear at a  corruption inquiry  but now the violence has also spread to  johannesburg  south africa's economic powerhouse with  police stretch thin  the army will deploy troops to the two  most affected regions  although these may be opportunistic acts  of  looting driven by hardship and poverty  the poor and the marginalized bear the  ultimate brunt of the destruction that  is  currently underway zuma is popular among  many of south africa's less well-off  his imprisonment has laid bare deep  divisions in the country
 

